=[twrit-rust]

`twrit-rust` -- Rust workspace rules enforcement (layering, door, purity).

==[Module api]

`rust-api` guard and documentation generator.

Extracts Tomet API documentation (`::kind(api)`) from Rust crates using `syn`,
traverses crate and module hierarchies, inspects item visibilities (`pub`),
and extracts doc-comments (`//!` and `///`).

Enforces that generated `.tmt` files in the repository match the current source
code and doc-comments.

===[Structs]

====[struct RustExtractOptions]

Options controlling extraction of Rust code documentation.

====[struct RustCrateSource]

In-memory representation of a Rust crate source code.

====[struct RustApiConfig]

Configuration for `rust-api` read from a Rule.

===[Functions]

====[fn extract_file_doc]

Extracts documentation for a single Rust source code string into a Tomet [`Document`].

====[fn extract_crate_doc]

Extracts documentation from an entire Rust crate into a single unified Tomet [`Document`].

====[fn format_document]

Formats a parsed AST [`Document`] into a formatted Tomet string.

====[fn load_workspace_crates]

Loads crate source files and workspace dependency graph using `cargo_metadata`.

====[fn check]

Guard check implementation for `rust-api`.

====[fn generate]

Generates and writes `.tmt` files for all crates, returning the count of written files.

====[fn generate_to]

Generates and writes `.tmt` files to `out_dir`.

==[Module door]

A capability's allowed locations, and everyone else held to not having
it.

The rule this exists for was written in prose and could not fail.
`tomet`'s `parser-purity` says file resolution is
"`tomet-semantics-resolver`'s job -- and the only place reading a
referenced file is allowed". Five crates touch `fs`. The sentence was
true when it was written, a second place appeared, and nothing
reported it, because a sentence has no way to notice.

The difference from `pure` is the quantifier, and it is the whole
point. `pure` checks the crates it names: a crate that acquires an
ability without being named stays invisible to it. `door` names the
crates that @em[may] have the ability and checks every other member of
the workspace, so a new one arriving is what fails.

===[Functions]

====[fn check]

==[Module layering]

The `crate-layering` guard.

Two checks over one `layers` declaration:

1. @strong[Exhaustiveness] -- every workspace member matches exactly one
layer. This is the half that catches a rule that was never written,
rather than one that was broken, and it is why the layering rule is
worth guarding before any other.
2. @strong[Direction] -- a member's `[dependencies]` may only resolve to its
own layer or below.

`[dev-dependencies]` are exempt, because a crate reaching up to the
parser to test itself says nothing about how the shipped code is
arranged.

===[Functions]

====[fn check]

Runs the guard against the workspace whose manifest is at
`workspace_root/Cargo.toml`.

`Outcome::NotDeclared` when no rule guards `layers` -- not an empty
violation list, which would be indistinguishable from a workspace
whose every edge runs the right way.

==[Module metadata]

===[Enums]

====[enum Flavour]

===[Functions]

====[fn get]

Fetches cargo metadata for the given manifest path and flavour, using the
cache if already queried for this manifest and flavour.

An error during `cargo metadata` execution is also cached and returned on
subsequent calls, ensuring errors are reported consistently.

==[Module purity]

The `pure` guard: crates that may not reach the outside world.

Two halves, because there are two ways to acquire the ability:

1. @strong[`allow-external`] -- every external crate in the dependency
closure is named there. Something new cannot arrive without a
decision, which is the property; "no external crates at all" would be
a nicer sentence and a false one.
2. @strong[`forbid`] -- none of the named paths appears in the crate's
source, so it cannot be done bare-handed either.

Neither name nor path is written here. Which crates, and which paths,
are `pure` data in the writ -- see `AGENTS.md`'s "What belongs in this
tool".

===[Functions]

====[fn check]

Runs every `pure` declared across `writs`.

`Outcome::NotDeclared` when no writ carries one, for the same reason
`crate-layering` does: an empty violation list is what a guard that
checked everything returns, and what one that checked nothing
returns, and they are not the same answer.

